Portable fixed point (UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer clean)
-------------------------------------------------------

The integer GSM 06.10 coder/decoder and the WAV49 packing of the same
integer algorithm are written as strictly defined C for two's complement
machines with 8 bit bytes, 16 bit short, 32 bit int and a long that may
be 32 or 64 bits.  The FAST/USE_FLOAT_MUL and LTP_CUT approximation
branches are untouched; gsm.h, gsm_signal, the opaque state and the
33 byte normal frame are unchanged.

What was actually wrong, and what the rewrite removes
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

These were genuine undefined or implementation-defined operations in
the integer chain, not stylistic issues:

  * Negative values shifted left.  C only defines << on non-negative
    signed operands.  Sites included the LAR reconstruction in
    short_term.c, the input downscale and the s1/msp placement in
    preprocess.c, the autocorrelation scaling/normalization and the LAR
    transformation in lpc.c, the RPE grid energies and APCM
    quantization/dequantization in rpe.c, and the cross-correlation,
    power and normalization shifts in long_term.c.
  * Relying on >> of a negative signed value being arithmetic.  The code
    assumed round-toward-minus-infinity, which is not guaranteed by C and
    is different from the truncation of signed division.
  * Assuming long is exactly 32 bits and that signed overflow wraps.

The implementation now keeps four operation classes distinct:

  * Internal 16/32 bit signed quantities stay in their declared words.
  * Wide intermediate products use the natural longword product; a
    16x16 product cannot overflow 32 bits.
  * Saturating add/sub keep the GSM clamp rules (including the MIN_WORD
    -> MAX_WORD absolute value rule) and are computed without signed
    overflow, using unsigned magnitude bookkeeping for the 32 bit cases.
  * Deliberate low-bit truncations (the gsm_asl/gsm_L_asl word shifts)
    explicitly keep the destination word's low bits and re-interpret
    them as two's complement.  Their historical behavior is preserved:
    shifts at or beyond the word width return 0, and negative counts
    reverse into an arithmetic right shift (and vice versa).

Arithmetic right shifts use the floor identity
floor(x / 2^n) = ~((~x) >> n) for x < 0, which only ever shifts
non-negative operands, so it neither assumes a hardware arithmetic
shift nor substitutes C's toward-zero division.  The scaling positions
in the MAC/LAR/normalization/RPE-dequantization paths are unchanged;
only the representation of each shift was made explicit.

Basis for compatibility (no single hash is claimed as proof)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The observable codec output is unchanged.  This is established several
independent ways, each reproduced at -O0 and -O2 and with and without
the SASR define, under full -fsanitize=undefined:

  * The fixed devcheck baseline still prints its exact compressed-byte
    and PCM fingerprints and non-zero count over the same 12 frames.
  * A 500 frame diverse stream (silence, both rails, random rail flips,
    full-range noise, small and mid-scale signals) is bit-identical to
    the unmodified library at both the 33 byte frame level and the
    decoded PCM level.
  * The add-test arithmetic vectors (add, sub, mult, mult_r, L_mult,
    L_add, L_sub, abs, norm, shifts and div) produce identical results.
  * tests/portable_fixed.c independently reconstructs expected values
    from long long arithmetic and an explicit quotient/remainder floor
    rule (never from a production helper), and checks whole-codec
    streams, hand-built legal parameter frames (edge LAR, RPE pulses,
    every grid, long-term lag reuse) and normal-vs-WAV49 behavior.

The WAV49 half-byte chaining state machine is preserved verbatim, so its
per-frame output matches the original implementation even where WAV49
does not byte-equal the normal 33 byte packing.

Usage and verification
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Plain encode/decode, and the WAV49 option, using only the compiler and
the standard library:

    #include "gsm.h"

    gsm enc = gsm_create(), dec = gsm_create();
    gsm_signal in[160], out[160];
    gsm_byte frame[33];

    gsm_encode(enc, in, frame);          /* 160 samples -> 33 bytes */
    if (gsm_decode(dec, frame, out) != 0)
        /* bad frame magic */;

    int on = 1;
    gsm_option(enc, GSM_OPT_WAV49, &on); /* same integer algorithm,    */
    gsm_option(dec, GSM_OPT_WAV49, &on); /* WAV49 65-byte frame pairs  */

    gsm_destroy(enc);
    gsm_destroy(dec);

Run the complete matrix (four O0/O2 by SASR combinations with full
UBSan, then the normal non-sanitized baseline) with:

    bash devcheck/target.sh

